{"\ufeffFAIRNESS Domain Alignment Analysis\n## FA-1: Measures to identify and mitigate biases\nAlignment Overview: This question addresses bias identification and mitigation, core to ethical AI.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports the Fairness principle by ensuring AI systems avoid discrimination.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Measures should be taken to ensure the AI produced decisions are compliant with anti-discrimination laws.\" (Fairness principle, paragraph 3)\n---\n## FA-2: Ensuring training data represents demographics\nAlignment Overview: Assesses whether AI systems use representative training data.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Addresses a root cause of unfair AI systems\u2014biased training data.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This is particularly important given concerns about the potential for AI to perpetuate societal injustices and have a disparate impact on vulnerable and underrepresented groups including, but not limited to, groups relating to age, disability, race, sex, intersex status, gender identity and sexual orientation.\" (Fairness principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## FA-3: Evaluating outputs for disparities\nAlignment Overview: Examines whether organizations test for unequal outcomes across user groups.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Promotes evaluation to detect unfair discrimination in AI outputs.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should be inclusive and accessible, and should not involve or result in unfair discrimination against individuals, communities or groups.\" (Fairness principle, main statement)\n---\n## FA-4: Measuring fairness in outputs\nAlignment Overview: Focuses on how organizations define and measure fairness metrics.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Enables quantifiable assessment of fairness in AI systems.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Measures should be taken to ensure the AI produced decisions are compliant with anti-discrimination laws.\" (Fairness principle, paragraph 3)\n---\n## FA-5: Testing for biases in real-world scenarios\nAlignment Overview: Assesses testing in real-world conditions to identify practical biases.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Ensures fairness is evaluated in actual deployment contexts.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes both appropriate consultation with stakeholders, who may be affected by the AI system throughout its lifecycle, and ensuring people receive equitable access and treatment.\" (Fairness principle, paragraph 1)\n---\nFA-6: Using frameworks to assess bias\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates use of structured approaches for bias detection and mitigation.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Promotes systematic fairness assessment using established tools.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Measures should be taken to ensure the AI produced decisions are compliant with anti-discrimination laws.\" (Fairness principle, paragraph 3)\n---\n## FA-7: Fairness for minority or underserved groups\nAlignment Overview: Specifically addresses fairness for vulnerable populations.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly addresses concerns about disparate impacts on underrepresented groups.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This is particularly important given concerns about the potential for AI to perpetuate societal injustices and have a disparate impact on vulnerable and underrepresented groups including, but not limited to, groups relating to age, disability, race, sex, intersex status, gender identity and sexual orientation.\" (Fairness principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## FA-8: Embedding fairness in design\nAlignment Overview: Examines whether fairness is proactive or reactive in the AI lifecycle.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports lifecycle approach to fairness emphasized in the principles.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Throughout their lifecycle, AI systems should be inclusive and accessible, and should not involve or result in unfair discrimination against individuals, communities or groups.\" (Fairness principle, main statement)\n---\n## TR-1: Level of detail about functionality and limitations\nAlignment Overview: Assesses how organizations communicate AI capabilities and boundaries.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports transparency about what the system can and cannot do.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Achieving transparency in AI systems through responsible disclosure is important to each stakeholder group for the following reasons: for users, what the system is doing and why...\" (Transparency principle, stakeholder disclosure section)\n---\n## TR-2: Documenting data sources and algorithms\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates documentation practices for AI components.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Enables transparency about data and algorithmic foundations.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Achieving transparency in AI systems through responsible disclosure is important... for creators, including those undertaking the validation and certification of AI, the systems' processes and input data...\" (Transparency principle, stakeholder disclosure section)\n---\n## TR-3: Mechanisms for external parties\nAlignment Overview: Assesses how external stakeholders can understand AI systems.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Enables transparency for regulators, auditors, and the public.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Achieving transparency in AI systems through responsible disclosure is important... for regulators in the context of investigations": null, " for those in the legal process, to inform evidence and decision-making": null, " for the public, to build confidence in the technology.\" (Transparency principle, stakeholder disclosure section)\n---\n## TR-4: Sharing AI system information\nAlignment Overview: Examines communication practices about AI systems.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Promotes effective sharing of AI information with stakeholders.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Responsible disclosures should be provided in a timely manner, and provide reasonable justifications for AI systems outcomes.\" (Transparency principle, paragraph following stakeholder list)\n---\n## TR-5: Black-box components impeding transparency\nAlignment Overview: Identifies transparency barriers in AI systems.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Addresses challenges to meaningful transparency.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle also aims to ensure people have the ability to find out when an AI system is engaging with them (regardless of the level of impact), and are able to obtain a reasonable disclosure regarding the AI system.\" (Transparency principle, final paragraph)\n---\n## TR-6: Disclosing data and algorithm sources\nAlignment Overview: Examines transparency about data and algorithm origins.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports disclosure about AI system foundations.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Achieving transparency in AI systems through responsible disclosure is important... for creators, including those undertaking the validation and certification of AI, the systems' processes and input data...\" (Transparency principle, stakeholder disclosure section)\n---\n## TR-7: Awareness of AI interaction\nAlignment Overview: Assesses whether users know when they interact with AI.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly addresses principle requirement for disclosure of AI engagement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle also aims to ensure people have the ability to find out when an AI system is engaging with them (regardless of the level of impact), and are able to obtain a reasonable disclosure regarding the AI system.\" (Transparency principle, final paragraph)\n---\n## TR-8: Publishing transparency reports\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates formal reporting on AI systems.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Promotes structured disclosure of AI information.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Responsible disclosures should be provided in a timely manner, and provide reasonable justifications for AI systems outcomes. This includes information that helps people understand outcomes, like key factors used in decision making.\" (Transparency principle, paragraph following stakeholder list)\n---\n## EX-1: Determining explainability level\nAlignment Overview: Addresses how organizations determine appropriate explainability.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports tailored explanations based on impact and stakeholder needs.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Responsible disclosures should be provided in a timely manner, and provide reasonable justifications for AI systems outcomes.\" (Transparency principle, paragraph following stakeholder list)\n---\n## EX-2: Testing explanation understandability\nAlignment Overview: Assesses whether explanations are comprehensible to non-experts.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Ensures explanations fulfill their purpose of creating understanding.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Achieving transparency in AI systems through responsible disclosure is important to each stakeholder group for the following reasons: for users, what the system is doing and why...\" (Transparency principle, stakeholder disclosure section)\n---\n## EX-3: Explanations in different formats\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates explanation adaptability for different audiences.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports tailored explanations for diverse stakeholders.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Achieving transparency in AI systems through responsible disclosure is important to each stakeholder group...\" with different requirements listed for users, creators, operators, investigators, regulators, legal process, and public. (Transparency principle, stakeholder disclosure section)\n---\n## EX-4: Evaluating explanation accuracy\nAlignment Overview: Examines the reliability of AI explanations.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Ensures explanations reflect actual system behavior.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Responsible disclosures should be provided in a timely manner, and provide reasonable justifications for AI systems outcomes.\" (Transparency principle, paragraph following stakeholder list)\n---\n## EX-5: Model complexity vs. explainability trade-offs\nAlignment Overview: Addresses balance between performance and explainability.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Acknowledges practical challenges in explanation implementation.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes information that helps people understand outcomes, like key factors used in decision making.\" (Transparency principle, paragraph following stakeholder list)\n---\n## EX-6: Ensuring decisions are interpretable\nAlignment Overview: Focuses on making AI decisions understandable to users.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports the goal of helping people understand AI impacts.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Responsible disclosures should be provided in a timely manner, and provide reasonable justifications for AI systems outcomes. This includes information that helps people understand outcomes, like key factors used in decision making.\" (Transparency principle, paragraph following stakeholder list)\n---\n## EX-7: Tools for complex model explainability\nAlignment Overview: Assesses use of technologies to explain complex models.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Addresses technical mechanisms to achieve explanation goals.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes information that helps people understand outcomes, like key factors used in decision making.\" (Transparency principle, paragraph following stakeholder list)\n---\n## EX-8: Tailoring explanations for specific groups\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates customization of explanations for different audiences.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Addresses the diverse needs of different stakeholders.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Achieving transparency in AI systems through responsible disclosure is important to each stakeholder group for the following reasons: for users... for creators... for those deploying and operating the system... for an accident investigator... for regulators... for those in the legal process... for the public...\" (Transparency principle, stakeholder disclosure section)\n---\n## AC-1: Managing accountability for AI failures\nAlignment Overview: Addresses responsibility when AI systems fail.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports accountability for AI outcomes.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle aims to acknowledge the relevant organisations' and individuals' responsibility for the outcomes of the AI systems that they design, develop, deploy and operate.\" (Accountability principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## AC-2: Governance structures for monitoring\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates formal oversight of AI systems.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Establishes mechanisms for ongoing accountability.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Mechanisms should be put in place to ensure responsibility and accountability for AI systems and their outcomes. This includes both before and after their design, development, deployment and operation.\" (Accountability principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## AC-3: Escalating issues policies\nAlignment Overview: Examines processes for addressing AI problems.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Ensures problems are appropriately handled.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"The organisation and individual accountable for the decision should be identifiable as necessary. They must consider the appropriate level of human control or oversight for the particular AI system or use case.\" (Accountability principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## AC-4: Handling complaints related to AI outputs\nAlignment Overview: Addresses response to customer concerns about AI decisions.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports both Accountability and Contestability principles.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle aims to ensure the provision of efficient, accessible mechanisms that allow people to challenge the use or output of an AI system, when that AI system significantly impacts a person, community, group or environment.\" (Contestability principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## AC-5: Defining AI governance roles\nAlignment Overview: Assesses clarity of AI responsibility assignments.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Establishes clear accountability for AI systems.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"The organisation and individual accountable for the decision should be identifiable as necessary.\" (Accountability principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## AC-6: Monitoring throughout lifecycle\nAlignment Overview: Examines ongoing oversight throughout AI development.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports lifecycle approach to accountability.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Mechanisms should be put in place to ensure responsibility and accountability for AI systems and their outcomes. This includes both before and after their design, development, deployment and operation.\" (Accountability principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## AC-7: Maintaining audit trails\nAlignment Overview: Addresses record-keeping for AI decisions.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Enables verification and oversight of AI behavior.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems that have a significant impact on an individual's rights should be accountable to external review, this includes providing timely, accurate, and complete information for the purposes of independent oversight bodies.\" (Accountability principle, paragraph 3)\n---\n## AC-8: Addressing disputes about outcomes\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates mechanisms to challenge AI decisions.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports the Contestability principle.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle aims to ensure the provision of efficient, accessible mechanisms that allow people to challenge the use or output of an AI system... Knowing that redress for harm is possible, when things go wrong, is key to ensuring public trust in AI.\" (Contestability principle, paragraphs 1-2)\n---\n## DI-1: Validating data accuracy and quality\nAlignment Overview: Assesses practices to ensure high-quality data for AI.\nAlignment Type: Partially Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Contributes to reliability but lacks explicit mention in principles.\nConfidence Level: Medium\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring AI systems are reliable, accurate and reproducible as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## DI-2: Managing data lineage\nAlignment Overview: Addresses tracking of data origin and transformations.\nAlignment Type: Partially Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports accountability but isn't explicitly mentioned.\nConfidence Level: Medium\nCitation: \"AI systems that have a significant impact on an individual's rights should be accountable to external review, this includes providing timely, accurate, and complete information...\" (Accountability principle, paragraph 3)\n---\n## DI-3: Governance policies for data\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates formal data management policies.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports data governance requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring proper data governance, and management, for all data used and generated by the AI system throughout its lifecycle.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## DI-4: Ensuring up-to-date data\nAlignment Overview: Addresses data currency and freshness.\nAlignment Type: Partially Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Contributes to reliability but isn't explicitly mentioned.\nConfidence Level: Medium\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring AI systems are reliable, accurate and reproducible as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## DI-5: Addressing missing data\nAlignment Overview: Examines handling of incomplete data.\nAlignment Type: Partially Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Contributes to reliability but isn't explicitly mentioned.\nConfidence Level: Medium\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring AI systems are reliable, accurate and reproducible as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## DI-6: Managing data preprocessing\nAlignment Overview: Addresses data cleaning and preparation.\nAlignment Type: Partially Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Contributes to reliability but isn't explicitly mentioned.\nConfidence Level: Medium\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring AI systems are reliable, accurate and reproducible as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## DI-7: Evaluating third-party data sources\nAlignment Overview: Assesses vetting of external data providers.\nAlignment Type: Partially Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports data governance but isn't explicitly mentioned.\nConfidence Level: Medium\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring proper data governance, and management, for all data used and generated by the AI system throughout its lifecycle.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## DI-8: Tracking data lineage\nAlignment Overview: Focuses on data traceability across the AI lifecycle.\nAlignment Type: Partially Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports accountability but isn't explicitly mentioned.\nConfidence Level: Medium\nCitation: \"This includes providing timely, accurate, and complete information for the purposes of independent oversight bodies.\" (Accountability principle, paragraph 3)\n---\n## RE-1: Testing consistent performance\nAlignment Overview: Addresses reliability testing across conditions.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports reliable operation requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle aims to ensure that AI systems reliably operate in accordance with their intended purpose throughout their lifecycle. This includes ensuring AI systems are reliable, accurate and reproducible as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## RE-2: Stress testing for robustness\nAlignment Overview: Evaluates system performance under extreme conditions.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports testing requirements for reliability.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should be monitored and tested to ensure they continue to meet their intended purpose...\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## RE-3: Monitoring for model drift\nAlignment Overview: Addresses detection of performance degradation.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports ongoing monitoring requirement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should be monitored and tested to ensure they continue to meet their intended purpose, and any identified problems should be addressed with ongoing risk management as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## RE-4: Defining reliability success metrics\nAlignment Overview: Addresses how reliability is measured.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Establishes criteria for reliable operation.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring AI systems are reliable, accurate and reproducible as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## RE-5: Identifying failure points\nAlignment Overview: Examines proactive identification of vulnerabilities.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports risk assessment aspect of reliability.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should not pose unreasonable safety risks, and should adopt safety measures that are proportionate to the magnitude of potential risks... any identified problems should be addressed with ongoing risk management as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## RE-6: Ensuring consistent performance\nAlignment Overview: Focuses on reliability across varying conditions.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports reliable operation requirement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle aims to ensure that AI systems reliably operate in accordance with their intended purpose throughout their lifecycle. This includes ensuring AI systems are reliable, accurate and reproducible as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## RE-7: Detecting system downtime\nAlignment Overview: Addresses monitoring for failures.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports ongoing monitoring requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should be monitored and tested to ensure they continue to meet their intended purpose, and any identified problems should be addressed with ongoing risk management as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## RE-8: Testing and retraining frequency\nAlignment Overview: Addresses ongoing system maintenance.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports testing and monitoring requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should be monitored and tested to ensure they continue to meet their intended purpose, and any identified problems should be addressed with ongoing risk management as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SE-1: Protecting models from unauthorized access\nAlignment Overview: Addresses AI system security measures.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports security requirements for AI systems.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle also aims to ensure appropriate data and AI system security measures are in place.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SE-2: Handling adversarial risks\nAlignment Overview: Examines defenses against malicious attacks.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly addresses adversarial attack resilience.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes the identification of potential security vulnerabilities, and assurance of resilience to adversarial attacks.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SE-3: Security audits and penetration testing\nAlignment Overview: Assesses security testing practices.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports vulnerability identification requirement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes the identification of potential security vulnerabilities, and assurance of resilience to adversarial attacks.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SE-4: Securing model throughout lifecycle\nAlignment Overview: Addresses security across development stages.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports lifecycle approach to security.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Throughout their lifecycle, AI systems should respect and uphold privacy rights and data protection, and ensure the security of data.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, main statement)\n---\n## SE-5: Preventing unauthorized modifications\nAlignment Overview: Examines safeguards against tampering.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports security and resilience requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes the identification of potential security vulnerabilities, and assurance of resilience to adversarial attacks... Security measures should account for unintended applications of AI systems, and potential abuse risks, with appropriate mitigation measures.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SE-6: Protection against adversarial attacks\nAlignment Overview: Addresses defenses against input manipulation.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly addresses adversarial attack resilience.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes the identification of potential security vulnerabilities, and assurance of resilience to adversarial attacks.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SE-7: Protecting sensitive data\nAlignment Overview: Examines data protection in AI pipelines.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports data protection requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Throughout their lifecycle, AI systems should respect and uphold privacy rights and data protection, and ensure the security of data.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, main statement)\n---\n## SE-8: Regular vulnerability assessments\nAlignment Overview: Addresses ongoing security testing.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports vulnerability identification requirement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes the identification of potential security vulnerabilities, and assurance of resilience to adversarial attacks.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## PR-1: Ensuring compliance with privacy laws\nAlignment Overview: Addresses legal compliance with privacy regulations.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports privacy rights requirement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Throughout their lifecycle, AI systems should respect and uphold privacy rights and data protection, and ensure the security of data.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, main statement)\n---\n## PR-2: Techniques to protect personal data\nAlignment Overview: Examines methods for protecting personal information.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports data protection requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring proper data governance, and management, for all data used and generated by the AI system throughout its lifecycle. For example, maintaining privacy through appropriate data anonymisation where used by AI systems.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## PR-3: Managing user consent\nAlignment Overview: Addresses obtaining permission for data use.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports respecting privacy rights.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Throughout their lifecycle, AI systems should respect and uphold privacy rights and data protection...\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, main statement)\n---\n## PR-4: Preventing data use beyond purpose\nAlignment Overview: Examines limiting data use to stated purposes.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports proper data governance requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This includes ensuring proper data governance, and management, for all data used and generated by the AI system throughout its lifecycle.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## PR-5: Evaluating privacy protection effectiveness\nAlignment Overview: Addresses assessment of privacy measures.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports security and mitigation requirements.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Security measures should account for unintended applications of AI systems, and potential abuse risks, with appropriate mitigation measures.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## PR-6: Monitoring for privacy violations\nAlignment Overview: Examines detection of privacy breaches.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports ongoing privacy protection.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Throughout their lifecycle, AI systems should respect and uphold privacy rights and data protection, and ensure the security of data.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, main statement)\n---\n## PR-7: Evaluating privacy risks across lifecycle\nAlignment Overview: Addresses privacy assessment throughout development.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports lifecycle approach to privacy.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Throughout their lifecycle, AI systems should respect and uphold privacy rights and data protection, and ensure the security of data.\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, main statement)\n---\n## PR-8: Addressing cross-border data protection\nAlignment Overview: Examines international privacy compliance.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports comprehensive privacy protection.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"Throughout their lifecycle, AI systems should respect and uphold privacy rights and data protection...\" (Privacy Protection and Security principle, main statement)\n---\n## SA-1: Conducting safety impact assessments\nAlignment Overview: Addresses formal safety risk evaluation.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports safety risk assessment requirement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should not pose unreasonable safety risks, and should adopt safety measures that are proportionate to the magnitude of potential risks.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SA-2: Preventing unintended harm\nAlignment Overview: Examines measures to avoid harmful outcomes.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Directly supports safety risk mitigation.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should not pose unreasonable safety risks, and should adopt safety measures that are proportionate to the magnitude of potential risks.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SA-3: Monitoring for unsafe behavior\nAlignment Overview: Addresses real-time safety monitoring.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports ongoing monitoring requirement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should be monitored and tested to ensure they continue to meet their intended purpose, and any identified problems should be addressed with ongoing risk management as appropriate.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SA-4: Handling conflicts with human safety\nAlignment Overview: Addresses prioritizing human wellbeing.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports human-centered values principle.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"This principle aims to ensure that AI systems are aligned with human values. Machines should serve humans, and not the other way around.\" (Human-centered Values principle, paragraph 1)\n---\n## SA-5: Emergency shutdown protocols\nAlignment Overview: Examines procedures for halting dangerous systems.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports safety measures requirement.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should not pose unreasonable safety risks, and should adopt safety measures that are proportionate to the magnitude of potential risks.\" (Reliability and Safety principle, paragraph 2)\n---\n## SA-6: Identifying and mitigating risks\nAlignment Overview: Addresses proactive risk management.\nAlignment Type: Fully Aligns\nSupport for Principles: Supports risk assessment and management.\nConfidence Level: High\nCitation: \"AI systems should not pose unreasonable safety risks, and should adopt safety measures that are propor